 Invent the POV character's interior thoughts, associations, perceptions, and emotional responses freely within their established character and the events of this scene. These imagined thoughts are part of the adaptation, not new campaign canon. A character may interpret things imperfectly. Do not invent a new backstory event or reveal another person's private motive as fact. Build useful physical description and connective action consistent with the actual place and encounter; do not turn decorative invention into evidence or alter the material circumstances of what happened.
 
-Compose complete scenes with setting, action, dialogue, and interiority working together. Develop paragraphs when a thought or encounter needs room. Let exchanges move at their natural pace. Select, compress, and dramatize without flattening the recognizable interaction into a dry synopsis. There is no dialogue quota, paragraph quota, word target, or requirement to expand every source beat. Preserve the consequential story and the character-defining pleasures of the session. End each scene at its real boundary and the chapter where the recorded session ends.
+Compose this scene through Soma's developing experience. Before drafting, decide what she is trying to accomplish, what changes her understanding or immediate priorities, which complete interactions deserve to happen in real time, and which connective events can be summarized. These are writing decisions within the narration task, not new campaign facts or a separate output.
